Tracking is different from a prediction, there's data behind tracking, a prediction is a guess. It's funny how you take boxofficepro's prediction in such high standard but in reality they do whatever this sub does. Hell, I would take the average prediction of 100 redditors over boxofficepro's.

&amp;#x200B;

You still don't get my point, industry tracking is not expectations. If JL opened at what it was tracking at (110M), it would still be a flop and there's no way in hell WB expectations for a 300M movie was 110M OW. Just like Shazam opening slightly above projection doesn't mean that's what WB or majority of people were expecting when they were making this movie.

&amp;#x200B;

&gt;It would be very disappointing. That would be about 50% of tracking/industry expectations.

&amp;#x200B;

Dude, TRACKING is based on pre-sales and social media interest, if TFA was gonna open at 100M (and finish 800M) it would get different pre-sales, the tracking wouldn't be that high. Justice League was projected at 110M because the movie had bad pre-sales and social media interest, not because WB or anyone was expecting a 300M movie to open that low, tracking isn't EXPECTATIONS. Please tell me why TLJ is widely considered a disappointment in this sub, even tho it's the most profitable movie of 2017, easily tripled it's budget and opened far higher than it's tracking.

&amp;#x200B;

&gt;Because it made significantly less than industry expectations and tracking.

&amp;#x200B;

But it tripled it's budget? Isn't budget suppose to be tangible aspect of "expectations"? Or does this apply only to Shazam?

&amp;#x200B;

["The picture formally went on tracking Thursday, with services predicting a debut of between $190 million to $200 million"](https://variety.com/2015/film/box-office/avengers-age-of-ultron-box-office-record-1201468875/)

&amp;#x200B;

Not significantly.

&amp;#x200B;

&gt;A movie that will make in lines with expectations looks bad compared to wonderkind movies that made way higher than expectations. What a shocker?

&amp;#x200B;

It won't make in lines with expectations, that's the problem.
